 <tr><th>Header File</th><td>\#include "SoilTransportModel.h"</td></tr>
 <tr><th>Class</th><td>\ref SoilTransportModel</td></tr>
 </table>

 The plugin is organized around the high-level \ref SoilTransportModel facade, which owns the
 lower-level solver classes: \ref SoilColumn (a single 1D column), \ref SoilGrid (a 2D array of
 columns for the 1D and quasi-3D modes), \ref SoilSolver3D (the full 3D Richards solver), and
 \ref RootSoilCoupling (plant-root water uptake). Most users interact only with \ref SoilTransportModel.

 \section SWT_Constructor Class Constructor

 <table>
 <tr><th>Constructors</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\ref SoilTransportModel( helios::Context* )</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section SWT_Data Primitive Data

 The plugin operates on its own internal finite-volume grid and does <b>not</b> require any input
 primitive data from the Context. It only writes primitive data when the soil geometry is rendered
 with \ref SoilTransportModel::addSoilVisualization() / \ref SoilTransportModel::updateSoilVisualization().

 \subsection SWT_OutputData Output Primitive Data (visualization geometry)

| Primitive Data Label | Symbol | Units | Data Type | Description |
| -------------------- | ------ | ------ | --------- | ----------- |
| water_content        | \f$\theta\f$ | m\f$^3\f$ m\f$^{-3}\f$ | \htmlonly<span style="color: green;"><tt>float</tt></span>\endhtmlonly | Volumetric water content sampled onto each soil voxel |
| pressure_head        | \f$h\f$ | m | \htmlonly<span style="color: green;"><tt>float</tt></span>\endhtmlonly | Pressure head sampled onto each soil voxel |
| soil_sample_pos      | N/A | m | \htmlonly<span style="color: green;"><tt>vec3</tt></span>\endhtmlonly | Sample location used by \ref SoilTransportModel::updateSoilVisualization() to re-color existing geometry in place |

 \section SWT_Issues Known Issues

 - The <tt>NGS_FAS</tt> nonlinear method is semi-implicit in the hydraulic conductivity (lagged within
   each step), so its mass-conservation error grows with both timestep and grid refinement. Keep the
   timestep moderate (or use the adaptive stepper) when accurate conservation matters; use <tt>PICARD</tt>
   for machine-exact conservation.
 - The <tt>M_SCHEME</tt> (Kirchhoff-transform) method is restricted to <b>homogeneous</b> soil and will
   throw if heterogeneous properties are set.
 - The CPU solver synchronizes its OpenMP threads many times per iteration. By default it uses one thread
   per physical core, which leaves the hyperthreads free for other work, but if more processes than
   physical cores keep the machine busy, the spinning threads can slow it down many-fold. On a heavily
   shared machine set the environment variable <tt>OMP_WAIT_POLICY=passive</tt> (about 10% slower on an
   idle machine) or lower <tt>num_threads</tt>.
 - The GPU backend is double-precision throughout; commodity cards with a low FP64 ratio provide little
   speedup over a multicore CPU. An A100/H100-class device is recommended.
 - Surface ponding and overland flow are not yet modeled; infiltration in excess of the surface
   capacity is not routed laterally.
 - Explicit root geometry is not supported yet: <tt>setRootPrimitives()</tt> raises an error. Use
   <tt>setRootDistribution()</tt> or <tt>setRootDistributionWeibull()</tt> to describe a plant's root zone.

 \section SWT_Intro Introduction

 The SoilTransportModel plugin solves variably-saturated subsurface water flow (the Richards equation)
 in one, two-and-a-half (quasi-3D), or three dimensions. It is intended for coupling with plant models:
 drip or sprinkler irrigation, root water uptake, and bare-soil evaporation can all be applied as
 boundary conditions and sink terms, and point sensors can log water content or pressure head over time.

 Three solve modes are available through \ref SoilTransportModel::setSolverType():
 <ul>
 <li><b>SOLVER_1D</b> &mdash; a grid of independent vertical columns (fastest; no lateral flow).</li>
 <li><b>SOLVER_QUASI_3D</b> &mdash; vertical columns with periodic operator-split horizontal coupling.</li>
 <li><b>SOLVER_3D</b> &mdash; the fully coupled 3D Richards equation (\ref SoilSolver3D), with an optional GPU backend.</li>
 </ul>

 Soil hydraulic behavior follows the van Genuchten&ndash;Mualem model by default, with a Gardner
 exponential model available as an alternative. Twelve standard USDA soil textures are provided in a
 built-in library (see \ref SWT_Params).

 \section SWT_Equations Governing Equations

 \subsection SWT_Richards The Richards equation

 Water movement is governed by the mixed form of the Richards equation, written here with the vertical
 coordinate \f$z\f$ positive upward and pressure head \f$h\f$ (m):
 \f[
   \frac{\partial \theta(h)}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot \left[ K(h)\left(\nabla h + \hat{\mathbf{z}}\right) \right] - S,
 \f]
 where \f$\theta\f$ is the volumetric water content (m\f$^3\f$ m\f$^{-3}\f$), \f$K\f$ is the unsaturated
 hydraulic conductivity (m s\f$^{-1}\f$), and \f$S\f$ is a sink (root uptake / evaporation, s\f$^{-1}\f$).
 The \f$\hat{\mathbf{z}}\f$ term is the gravitational contribution.

 \subsection SWT_Constitutive Constitutive relations

 The van Genuchten&ndash;Mualem model relates effective saturation \f$S_e\f$, water content, and
 conductivity to pressure head. For \f$h<0\f$,
 \f[
   S_e(h) = \left[1 + |\alpha h|^{n}\right]^{-m}, \qquad m = 1 - \frac{1}{n},
 \f]
 with \f$S_e = 1\f$ for \f$h \ge 0\f$. Water content and conductivity follow as
 \f[
   \theta(h) = \theta_r + (\theta_s - \theta_r)\,S_e(h), \qquad
   K(S_e) = K_s\, S_e^{1/2}\left[1 - \left(1 - S_e^{1/m}\right)^{m}\right]^{2},
 \f]
 where \f$\theta_r\f$ and \f$\theta_s\f$ are the residual and saturated water contents, \f$\alpha\f$
 (m\f$^{-1}\f$) is the inverse air-entry scale, \f$n\f$ is the pore-size distribution index, and
 \f$K_s\f$ is the saturated conductivity. The specific moisture capacity used by the solvers is
 \f$C(h) = \mathrm{d}\theta/\mathrm{d}h\f$.

 \par Modified van Genuchten&ndash;Mualem model (air-entry value)

 For \f$n < 2\f$ the slope \f$\mathrm{d}K/\mathrm{d}h\f$ of the standard model is unbounded as
 \f$h \to 0^-\f$, so K drops very steeply just below saturation. Cells that sit at saturation, such as the
 cells under a drip emitter delivering more than \f$K_s\f$, can then stall the nonlinear solver.
 <tt>SoilModel::MODIFIED_VAN_GENUCHTEN</tt> (Ippisch, Vogel &amp; Bastian 2006) adds an air-entry head
 \f$h_e < 0\f$ (<tt>SoilProperties::h_e</tt>): the soil is saturated for \f$h \ge h_e\f$, and for \f$h < h_e\f$
 \f[
   S_e = \frac{\left[1 + |\alpha h|^{n}\right]^{-m}}{S_c}, \qquad
   K = K_s\, S_e^{1/2}\left[\frac{1 - \left(1 - (S_e S_c)^{1/m}\right)^{m}}{1 - \left(1 - S_c^{1/m}\right)^{m}}\right]^{2},
   \qquad S_c = \left[1 + |\alpha h_e|^{n}\right]^{-m},
 \f]
 so \f$\theta = \theta_s\f$ and \f$K = K_s\f$ at \f$h_e\f$, and \f$\mathrm{d}K/\mathrm{d}h\f$ stays finite. With
 the same parameters as a standard soil, below \f$h_e\f$ the water content is the standard one rescaled by
 \f$1/S_c\f$ and the conductivity the standard one times
 \f$F_K = 1/\left(S_c^{1/2}\left[1 - (1 - S_c^{1/m})^{m}\right]^2\right)\f$ (1.3 for sand, 1.7 for loam, 10 for clay
 at \f$h_e\f$ = -2 cm). \ref toModifiedVanGenuchten() therefore converts standard parameters by setting
 \f$\theta_s\f$ and \f$K_s\f$ to the standard \f$\theta\f$ and \f$K\f$ at \f$h_e\f$: the modified model is then
 the standard one truncated at \f$h_e\f$, identical for \f$h < h_e\f$ and saturated above. Passing
 <tt>match_standard_curves = false</tt> keeps \f$\theta_s\f$ and \f$K_s\f$ instead (HYDRUS's convention). The model
 is supported by the column, quasi-3D and 3D solvers on the CPU and GPU, except <tt>M_SCHEME</tt>.

 \par Relative-conductivity spline near saturation

 Alternatively the standard model can keep \f$K_s\f$ at saturation with a finite slope: with
 <tt>SoilProperties::kr_spline_start</tt> \f$= S_1 < 1\f$ (e.g. 0.9999), the relative conductivity above
 \f$S_e = S_1\f$ is a cubic Hermite spline from the standard value and slope at \f$S_1\f$ to 1 with zero slope at
 saturation. The retention curve is unchanged.

 A Gardner exponential soil, \f$K(h) = K_s\,e^{\alpha h}\f$, is also available via the
 <tt>gardner()</tt> named constructor of \ref SoilProperties; it admits closed-form
 steady-infiltration solutions used in the plugin's verification tests. A linear soil,
 \f$S_e = 1 + \alpha h\f$ (between 0 and 1) with \f$K = K_s S_e\f$, is available via the <tt>linear()</tt> named
 constructor, for verification against closed-form solutions.

 \subsection SWT_Discretization Discretization

 The domain is discretized with a cell-centered finite-volume scheme. Specified-head (Dirichlet)
 boundary faces are treated as a virtual cell half a cell beyond the face and use the same interblock
 weighting as interior faces, with flow direction set by total head. Interblock conductivities on cell
 faces are formed by harmonic, geometric, arithmetic, upstream, or upstream relative-permeability weighting
 (the <tt>K_weighting</tt> field of \ref SolverParameters). The default is the arithmetic mean, which in
 grid-convergence tests (infiltration into dry soil, ponded infiltration, layered recharge, evaporation and
 3D drip irrigation) was the most accurate overall at practical resolutions and never failed to converge;
 the harmonic mean nearly blocks infiltration from a wet cell into a dry one. Upstream weighting takes the
 conductivity of the donor cell (by total head). Upstream relative-permeability
 weighting (<tt>KWeighting::UPSTREAM_KR</tt>) multiplies the harmonic mean of the two cells'
 saturated conductivities by the donor cell's relative permeability (the scheme used by MODFLOW 6); it
 is the better choice across sharp layer interfaces, where taking the donor's full \f$K\f$ lets a coarse
 layer's \f$K_s\f$ control flow into a fine one. Time integration uses backward Euler, giving a nonlinear
 algebraic system at each step that is solved by one of the strategies below. Backward Euler conserves water
 exactly, whatever the step sizes. The 3D solver can use second-order BDF2 instead (SolverParameters::bdf2),
 but BDF2 does not conserve water when sources or boundary fluxes change: an emitter switched on and off under
 adaptive stepping lost 1.6% of its water, and with daily recharge over a year (MODFLOW 6 Test 2) the heads
 ended 29 cm low. Use BDF2 only with fixed steps and smooth forcing.

 The 1D column solver (\ref SoilColumn) uses the mass-conservative Modified-Picard form of Celia et al.
 (1990) with a direct tridiagonal solve.

 \subsection SWT_Solvers Nonlinear solvers (3D)

 The full-3D solver (\ref SoilSolver3D) offers several nonlinear strategies, selected with the
 <tt>nonlinear_method</tt> field of \ref SolverParameters. The available strategies are:
 <ul>
 <li><b>NEWTON_ANALYTIC</b> (default) &mdash; Newton&ndash;Raphson on an explicitly assembled 7-point
     Jacobian with analytic derivatives, including the \f$\mathrm{d}K/\mathrm{d}h\f$ terms that
     Modified Picard drops, so convergence is quadratic near the solution. The nonsymmetric linear system
     is solved with ILU(0)-preconditioned BiCGSTAB (on large grids, block-Jacobi ILU(0) with one lateral
     slab of whole vertical columns per thread). Robustness comes from limiting each cell's
     effective-saturation change per iteration (unsaturated cells take the update in water-content space;
     cells with specific storage near or below the water table take it in head) and a backtracking line
     search. A step converges when every cell's water imbalance is negligible, so mass is conserved to
     solver tolerance. Inside <tt>solveAdaptive()</tt> a step that fails to converge is retried with a
     smaller \f$\Delta t\f$; a fixed-\f$\Delta t\f$ <tt>solve()</tt> falls back to Modified Picard for
     that step on uniform grids and, if the step still fails, sub-steps the interval adaptively. Only if
     that also fails does it return 0, leaving the state unchanged. On the GPU the
     same algorithm runs device-resident, with BiCGSTAB preconditioned by vertical-line relaxation (flat or
     terrain-following cells, where vertical coupling dominates) or a geometric-multigrid V-cycle.</li>
 <li><b>PICARD</b> &mdash; the Modified-Picard iteration of Celia et al. (1990), which linearizes
     about \f$C(h)\f$ to yield a symmetric positive-definite system each iteration, solved by
     preconditioned conjugate gradient with geometric multigrid. Robust and machine-exact in mass; also the
     fallback of a failed NEWTON_ANALYTIC step.</li>
 <li><b>NEWTON</b> &mdash; a Jacobian-free Newton&ndash;Krylov (JFNK) scheme using matrix-free
     Jacobian&ndash;vector products and GMRES preconditioned by a single multigrid V-cycle. Roughly 35%
     faster than Picard on stiff dry-front steps.</li>
 <li><b>PICARD_NEWTON</b> &mdash; a Picard warm-up followed by Newton for additional globalization.</li>
 <li><b>NGS_FAS</b> &mdash; lagged-conductivity nonlinear Gauss&ndash;Seidel smoothing within a full
     approximation scheme (FAS) multigrid (Gu &amp; Wang, 2026); its operator uses harmonic-mean faces, so
     use it with <tt>KWeighting::HARMONIC</tt>. Iteration counts are grid-independent and
     the smoother is red-black parallel on the GPU; semi-implicit, so mass error is \f$O(\Delta t)\f$.</li>
 <li><b>M_SCHEME</b> &mdash; a Kirchhoff-transform linearization with a certified a-posteriori error
     estimator (Mitra &amp; Pop and co-workers, 2025). Homogeneous soil only; CPU only.</li>
 </ul>

 \par Failed steps (NEWTON_ANALYTIC, column and 3D solvers alike)

 The 1D column and the 3D solver (CPU and GPU) apply the same rules, with constants shared in SoilUtils.h:
 <ul>
 <li>A cell's residual is accepted when it is small in water-content units or relative to the sum of the
     magnitudes of its terms; that sum includes the round-off of each face flux, so cells far thinner than the
     head change across them (e.g. at a root surface) can still converge in double precision.</li>
 <li>The line search measures the residual in water-flux units (weighted by cell volume), so thin cells do not
     dominate it on graded grids.</li>
 <li>Newton gives up after ten damped iterations in a row that each remove less than 2% of the residual,
     the way it behaves when the step has no solution (e.g. a flux the soil can no longer deliver).</li>
 <li>A Picard step run as the fallback of a failed Newton step is accepted only if the water balance also closes.</li>
 <li><tt>solve(dt)</tt> rescues a failed step by sub-stepping it, down to 1/1000 of dt; if that cannot cover
     it, the state is left unchanged and 0 is returned, so the caller can shorten the step. A caller that shortens
     failed steps itself should set <tt>substep_on_failure = false</tt> (\ref SolverParameters, or
     \ref SoilSolver3D::Params): a step with no solution then fails at once instead of after the sub-steps.</li>
 <li>A failed step leaves the state unchanged with every nonlinear method. \ref SoilTransportModel::run() raises an
     error when it cannot complete a step, and its clock and the cumulative root uptake count only the time that was
     advanced. An error raised part-way through a step for another reason (e.g. a quasi-3D cell reaching saturation, or a
     GPU failure) leaves the state and the clock as they were before the call.</li>
 </ul>

 \par GPU
 The full 3D solver runs on an NVIDIA GPU automatically when the build has CUDA and a usable device is present
 (\ref SoilTransportModel::isGPUAvailable(); setting the environment variable <tt>HELIOS_NO_GPU</tt> hides it), except
 with <tt>NonlinearMethod::M_SCHEME</tt>, which runs on the CPU only. \ref SoilTransportModel::disableGPU() selects the CPU;
 \ref SoilTransportModel::enableGPU() requires the GPU. The column solvers always run on the CPU. A CUDA failure during a
 solve raises an error and leaves the state as it was before the step.

 \subsection SWT_Geometry Grid geometry, specified heads and specific storage (3D solver)

 By default each cell of \ref SoilSolver3D is a \f$dx \times dy \times dz\f$ box. Three options extend this
 for groundwater-style and terrain-following problems; all require <tt>NEWTON_ANALYTIC</tt> (CPU or GPU):
 <ul>
 <li><b>Per-cell geometry</b> (\ref SoilSolver3D::setCellGeometry): each cell gets its own centre elevation
     and thickness, so layers may slope and vary in thickness. Gravity uses the actual cell elevations;
     vertical faces use the centre-to-centre distance and lateral faces an area equal to the cell width
     times the mean thickness of the two cells.</li>
 <li><b>Specified-head cells</b> (\ref SoilSolver3D::setFixedHead): any cell can be held at a fixed pressure
     head (a canal, lake or water-table boundary on part of a face). The water such a cell exchanges with
     its neighbours is counted as external inflow in the mass balance.</li>
 <li><b>Specific storage</b> (\ref SoilSolver3D::setSpecificStorage): stored water per unit volume becomes
     \f$\theta(h) + S_s S_w h\f$ with \f$S_w = \theta/\theta_s\f$, so saturated cells store water as
     pressure rises. Even a small \f$S_s\f$ (\f$10^{-6}\f$--\f$10^{-4}\f$ m\f$^{-1}\f$) also makes the
     saturated zone far easier for the nonlinear solver.</li>
 </ul>

 \subsection SWT_Radial Radial geometry (1D column)

 \ref SoilColumn::setRadialGeometry() turns a \ref SoilColumn into concentric shells around a cylinder of given
 radius, such as a root: layer depths become radial distances from the cylinder surface, the top boundary is the
 cylinder surface and the bottom boundary the outer radius. Face fluxes scale with the face radius and layer
 storage with the shell area, and gravity is not applied (the flow is horizontal). Boundary fluxes, sources and
 water storage are per unit area of the cylinder surface (a bottom flux per unit area of the outer surface). This
 resolves the steep head drop toward a root in dry soil (rhizosphere models).

 \subsection SWT_Boundaries Boundary conditions and sinks

 Each boundary may be a specified pressure head (Dirichlet), a specified flux (Neumann,
 positive into the soil), or, at the base, unit-gradient free drainage. The top of a \ref SoilColumn may also
 be a head-limited ("atmospheric") boundary, as in HYDRUS (\ref SoilColumn::setTopBoundaryAtmospheric()): a
 potential flux is applied unless the flux with the surface held at a limiting head is smaller, i.e. infiltration
 limited by ponding at the surface or evaporation limited by a drying surface. The branch is chosen for each step
 from the state at its start and checked at its end (the step is redone with the other branch if needed);
 \ref SoilColumn::getTopFlux() returns the flux applied. Surface irrigation is applied
 with \ref SoilTransportModel::addSurfaceEmitter() (a drip emitter is spread over a small footprint for
 grid independence). Bare-soil evaporation (\ref SoilTransportModel::setSurfaceEvaporation()) and root
 uptake (\ref RootSoilCoupling) enter the equation through the sink term \f$S\f$; root uptake is
 partitioned over the soil cells according to a Vrugt-type or Weibull root-density profile.

 Root uptake is limited by soil water availability. Each root cell's share is scaled by a Feddes
 water-stress factor \f$\alpha(h)\f$, equal to 1 above \f$h_\mathrm{onset}\f$ (default \f$-4\f$ m) and
 falling linearly to 0 at \f$h_\mathrm{wilt}\f$ (default \f$-150\f$ m, about \f$-1.5\f$ MPa). Uptake is
 redistributed toward unstressed roots (Jarvis compensation), so the full transpiration demand is met while
 the root-weighted mean \f$\omega = \sum \alpha f / \sum f\f$ is at least \f$\omega_c\f$ (default 0.5); below
 that the uptake is \f$T\,\omega/\omega_c\f$. Set the parameters with
 \ref SoilTransportModel::setRootWaterStressParameters() and read the applied uptake with
 \ref SoilTransportModel::getRootUptakeFlux(). The root-zone water potential returned for plant-hydraulics
 coupling (\ref SoilTransportModel::getSoilWaterPotential()) is weighted the same way, by
 \f$f\,\alpha\f$, so it reflects the soil the roots are drawing from.

 \section SWT_Params Parameters

 \subsection SWT_SoilProps Soil hydraulic properties

 The \ref SoilProperties struct holds the van Genuchten&ndash;Mualem (or Gardner) parameters:

| Parameter | Symbol | Units | Description | Default (sand) |
| --------- | ------ | ----- | ----------- | -------------- |
| theta_r   | \f$\theta_r\f$ | m\f$^3\f$ m\f$^{-3}\f$ | Residual water content | 0.045 |
| theta_s   | \f$\theta_s\f$ | m\f$^3\f$ m\f$^{-3}\f$ | Saturated water content | 0.43 |
| alpha     | \f$\alpha\f$ | m\f$^{-1}\f$ | Inverse air-entry scale (van Genuchten \f$\alpha\f$ / Gardner rate) | 14.5 |
| n         | \f$n\f$ | &ndash; | Pore-size distribution index (van Genuchten only) | 2.68 |
| K_s       | \f$K_s\f$ | m s\f$^{-1}\f$ | Saturated hydraulic conductivity | \f$8.25\times10^{-5}\f$ |
| h_e       | \f$h_e\f$ | m | Air-entry head (<tt>MODIFIED_VAN_GENUCHTEN</tt> only, < 0) | 0 |

 Properties may be built directly, via the named constructors <tt>SoilProperties::vanGenuchten()</tt> /
 <tt>SoilProperties::modifiedVanGenuchten()</tt> / <tt>SoilProperties::gardner()</tt>, or looked up by USDA
 texture with \ref getSoilPropertiesFromTexture(). The library holds the Carsel &amp; Parrish (1988) class
 averages. <tt>getSoilPropertiesFromTexture(texture, SoilModel::MODIFIED_VAN_GENUCHTEN)</tt> (or
 <tt>SoilTransportModel::setSoilPropertiesFromTexture(texture, model)</tt>) converts them with
 \ref toModifiedVanGenuchten() at \f$h_e\f$ = -2 cm (the value of HYDRUS's "van Genuchten&ndash;Mualem with an
 air-entry value of -2 cm" option): the Carsel &amp; Parrish curves truncated at \f$h_e\f$. No class averages
 refitted for the air-entry model have been published for the 12 USDA classes; Schaap &amp; van Genuchten (2006)
 give 4 texture groups at -4 cm. Available textures:
 <tt>sand</tt>, <tt>loamy_sand</tt>, <tt>sandy_loam</tt>, <tt>loam</tt>, <tt>silt</tt>, <tt>silt_loam</tt>,
 <tt>sandy_clay_loam</tt>, <tt>clay_loam</tt>, <tt>silty_clay_loam</tt>, <tt>sandy_clay</tt>,
 <tt>silty_clay</tt>, and <tt>clay</tt>.

 \subsection SWT_SolverParams Solver parameters

 The \ref SolverParameters struct configures the nonlinear/linear solve and timestepping:

| Parameter | Units | Description | Default |
| --------- | ----- | ----------- | ------- |
| nonlinear_method | &ndash; | 3D nonlinear strategy (NEWTON_ANALYTIC, PICARD, NEWTON, PICARD_NEWTON, NGS_FAS, M_SCHEME) | NEWTON_ANALYTIC |
| max_picard_iterations | &ndash; | Maximum nonlinear iterations per step | 20 |
| picard_tolerance | m | Nonlinear convergence tolerance | \f$1\times10^{-5}\f$ |
| picard_omega | &ndash; | Under-relaxation factor (use 0.5&ndash;0.7 for sharp fronts) | 1.0 |
| anderson_depth | &ndash; | Anderson-acceleration depth for the Picard tail (0 = off) | 0 |
| K_weighting | &ndash; | Interblock conductivity weighting (DEFAULT/HARMONIC/GEOMETRIC/ARITHMETIC/UPSTREAM/UPSTREAM_KR) | DEFAULT (arithmetic) |
| K_min | m s\f$^{-1}\f$ | Additive conductivity floor easing dry-front infiltration (0 = off) | 0.0 |
| num_threads | &ndash; | CPU threads for the 3D solver (0 = <tt>OMP_NUM_THREADS</tt> if set, else one per physical core) | 0 |
| enable_gravity | &ndash; | Include gravity in the flux (column solvers only; false for horizontal or capillarity-only problems) | true |
| adaptive_dt | &ndash; | Enable \f$\Delta\theta\f$-controlled adaptive timestepping | false |
| dt_target_rel_change | &ndash; | Target per-step \f$|\Delta\theta|/(\theta_s-\theta_r)\f$ for step sizing | 0.03 |
| dt_min / dt_max | s | Timestep bounds | 0.01 / 3600 |
| horizontal_coupling_interval | &ndash; | Quasi-3D: couple laterally every N vertical steps | 1 |

 \section SWT_Example Examples

 \subsection SWT_Ex1 Minimal 3D infiltration

 A uniform, moderately dry loam with steady surface infiltration and free drainage at the base. The
 wetting front descends over the first hour.

 \code{.cpp}
 #include "SoilTransportModel.h"
 using namespace helios;

 int main() {
     Context context;
     SoilTransportModel soil(&context);

     // A 1 m x 1 m x 1 m block of soil, 20 x 20 x 40 cells.
     soil.defineSoilDomain(make_vec3(0, 0, 0), make_vec3(1, 1, 1));
     soil.setGridResolution(20, 20, 40);
     soil.setSolverType(SolverType::SOLVER_3D);

     soil.setSoilPropertiesFromTexture("loam");
     soil.setInitialCondition(-1.0f);              // uniform pressure head [m]
     soil.setTopBoundaryFlux(2e-6f);               // steady infiltration [m/s]
     soil.setBottomBoundaryFreeDrainage();

     for (int step = 0; step < 60; step++) {
         soil.run(60.0f);                          // advance 60 s
     }

     float theta = soil.getWaterContent(make_vec3(0.5f, 0.5f, -0.1f));
     std::cout << "Near-surface water content: " << theta << std::endl;
     std::cout << "Total storage [m^3]: " << soil.getTotalWaterStorage() << std::endl;
 }
 \endcode

 \subsection SWT_Ex2 Drip irrigation with root uptake and sensors

 Two drip emitters wet an initially dry loam while a plant transpires; a buried sensor logs the water
 content over the simulation and the series is written to CSV. The grid-independent, GPU-capable
 NGS-FAS solver is used with adaptive timestepping.

 \code{.cpp}
 #include "SoilTransportModel.h"
 using namespace helios;

 int main() {
     Context context;
     SoilTransportModel soil(&context);

     soil.defineSoilDomain(make_vec3(-0.5f, -0.5f, 0.0f), make_vec3(1, 1, 1.5f));
     soil.setGridResolution(24, 24, 32);
     soil.setSolverType(SolverType::SOLVER_3D);

     SolverParameters sp;
     sp.nonlinear_method = NonlinearMethod::NGS_FAS;
     sp.adaptive_dt = true;                        // sub-step through the stiff wetting front
     sp.dt_max = 3600.0f;
     sp.K_weighting = KWeighting::HARMONIC;        // NGS_FAS's operator uses harmonic-mean faces
     soil.setSolverParameters(sp);
     // soil.disableGPU();                         // the 3D solver uses a GPU when one is available

     soil.setSoilPropertiesFromTexture("loam");
     soil.setInitialCondition(-1.5f);              // moderately dry
     soil.setTopBoundaryFlux(0.0f);
     soil.setBottomBoundaryFreeDrainage();

     // Two drip emitters at the surface, ~2 L/h each.
     soil.addSurfaceEmitter(-0.25f, 0.0f, 5.6e-7f);
     soil.addSurfaceEmitter( 0.25f, 0.0f, 5.6e-7f);

     // A single transpiring plant with a default root distribution.
     RootDistributionParams roots;
     soil.setRootDistribution(/*plant_id=*/1, roots);
     soil.setTranspiration(1, 5e-7f);

     // A buried water-content sensor.
     uint sensor = soil.addSensor(make_vec3(0.0f, 0.0f, -0.2f), SoilMeasurement::WATER_CONTENT);

     for (int hour = 0; hour < 12; hour++) {
         soil.run(3600.0f);
         soil.recordSensors();
     }
     soil.writeSensorData("soil_moisture.csv");

     std::cout << "Sensor " << sensor << " logged " << soil.getSensorData(sensor).size()
               << " samples; root-weighted soil water potential is "
               << soil.getSoilWaterPotential(1) << " m." << std::endl;
 }
 \endcode

